<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tum Collegiate Church</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tum Collegiate Church, in the village of Tum near Łęczyca in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, is one of the largest surviving Romanesque churches in the country. Built of granite and sandstone, it was consecrated in 1161 on the site of an earlier Benedictine abbey, and it has two west towers and apses at both ends. Badly damaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, it was restored and remains a major monument of Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque architecture</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tum-collegiate-church.jpg" alt="Tum Collegiate Church" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sławomir Milejski · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SM_Tum_kolegiata_ID_612614.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
